'''yamadou''' (yam′ạ-dö), ''n.'' An oil obtained from the tallow-nutmeg, ''Myristica sebifera.'' See ''nutmeg'', 2.

'''yama-mai''' (yam′ä-mī′), ''n.'' [NL. (Guérin-Méneville, 1861), < Jap. ''yama-maï'', lit. ‘worm of the mountains.’] A large bombycid moth, whose larva feeds on the oak ''Quercus serrata'' in Japan, and furnishes silk of excellent quality which has long been utilized in the manufacture of the heavier native silk fabrics. {{smaller|The worm has been reared in Europe and in the United States, but has not been commercially successful in those countries.}}

