'''yammerly''' (yam′ėr-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''ȝamerly'', ''ȝomerly'', < AS. *''geómorlīce'', < ''geómorlīc'', lamentable, < ''geómor'', sad: see ''yammer'', ''v.''] Piteously. ''Gawayne.''

'''yamp''' (yamp), ''n.'' [N. Amer. Ind.] An umbelliferous plant, ''Carum Gairdneri'', found from California to Wyoming and Washington; doubtless, also, ''C. Kellogii'', of central California. These plants have fascicled tuberous roots, which are an important food of the Indians.

'''yamph''' (yamf), ''v. i.'' [Cf. ''yaff'', ''yap''<sup>1</sup>.] To bark continuously. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''yamun''' (yä′mun), ''n.'' [Chinese, < ''ya'', the marquee of a general, + ''mun'', a two-leaved door, a gate.] The official and private residence of a Chinese mandarin who holds a seal; the place where a mandarin transacts the business of the region or department under his care, and where he lives; a mandarin’s office, court, residence, etc.

{{smaller block|The three ''yamuns'' at our feet, with their quaint towers, grand old trees, flags, and the broad Pearl River on the other side of the city, are the only elements of positive beauty in the landscape.
{{right|''Lady Brassey'', Voyage of Sunbeam, II. xxii.}}}}

